  "Number": 10,
  "Question": "For \\(x\\) real, the inequality \\(1\\le\\lvert x-2\\rvert\\le7\\) is equivalent to:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x\\le1\\) or \\(x\\ge3\\)",
    "B": "\\(1\\le x\\le3\\)",
    "C": "\\(-5\\le x\\le9\\)",
    "D": "\\(-5\\le x\\le1\\) or \\(3\\le x\\le9\\)",
    "E": "\\(-6\\le x\\le1\\) or \\(3\\le x\\le10\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The quantity \\(\\lvert x-2\\rvert\\) is the distance from \\(x\\) to \\(2\\) on the number line. That distance must be at least \\(1\\) and at most \\(7\\).\n\nTo the left of \\(2\\), the allowed points run from \\(2-7=-5\\) to \\(2-1=1\\). To the right, they run from \\(2+1=3\\) to \\(2+7=9\\). Every point in these intervals has an allowed distance, and every point outside them violates one of the bounds.\n\nAll four endpoints are included because both inequalities allow equality. Thus\n\\[\n-5\\le x\\le1\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n3\\le x\\le9,\n\\]\nwhich is choice D.",
